A model registry from the MLOps course tracks one thing per deployment: which model is live. A prompt registry has to track something that changes far more often, so it needs its own history instead of a single overwritable slot:

```python
class PromptRegistry:
    """Tracks every version of a prompt, the way MLOps tracks model versions --
    but built for changes that happen far more often than a retrain."""

    def __init__(self):
        self._history = {}  # prompt_name -> list of version dicts

    def register(self, prompt_name: str, text: str, author: str) -> int:
        versions = self._history.setdefault(prompt_name, [])
        version_id = len(versions) + 1
        versions.append({"version": version_id, "text": text, "author": author})
        return version_id

    def get_active(self, prompt_name: str) -> dict:
        return self._history[prompt_name][-1]

    def rollback(self, prompt_name: str, version_id: int) -> int:
        target = next(v for v in self._history[prompt_name] if v["version"] == version_id)
        # Re-register rather than delete, so the full history stays intact
        return self.register(prompt_name, target["text"], author="rollback")
```

`register()` never overwrites a slot the way a single "current model" pointer might — every call appends, so the registry can answer "what did this prompt look like last Tuesday" the same way a model registry answers "what model was live last Tuesday." `rollback()` is deliberately implemented as another `register()` call: reverting is just shipping an old version again, which keeps the history linear and auditable instead of quietly deleting entries.
